<div class="header"><h2>why call it project achilles</h2></div>
<div class="body">
<p>In Greek mythology, <strong>Achilles</strong> was the greatest warrior of the Trojan War. His mother, the sea nymph Thetis, sought to make him immortal by dipping him as an infant into the River Styx &mdash; the waters that granted invulnerability. However, she held him by his heel, leaving that one spot vulnerable <sup><a href="#src-achilles-1">104</a>, <a href="#src-achilles-2">105</a>, <a href="#src-achilles-3">106</a></sup>.</p>
<p>An ancient prophecy foretold Achilles' fate: his mother was told he would die either from a long, peaceful life or from a brief but glorious warrior's death in battle. Achilles chose glory <sup><a href="#src-achilles-1">104</a></sup>. During the Trojan War, the god Apollo &mdash; who sided with Troy &mdash; guided Paris's arrow to strike Achilles in the only vulnerable spot &mdash; his heel &mdash; killing him <sup><a href="#src-achilles-4">107</a></sup>. Thus, the "Achilles heel" became a metaphor for a hidden weakness in an otherwise formidable defense.</p>
<p>How did Apollo know about Achilles' heel? In Greek mythology, prophecies were heard among the gods; the prophecy about Achilles' fate was no secret <sup><a href="#src-achilles-2">105</a>, <a href="#src-achilles-5">108</a></sup>. Additionally, as the god of prophecy himself, Apollo had access to divine knowledge beyond mortal understanding <sup><a href="#src-achilles-3">106</a></sup>. Some accounts also suggest Thetis confided Achilles' vulnerability to her husband Peleus, and the information spread through the divine court <sup><a href="#src-achilles-1">104</a></sup>.</p>
<p>We chose this name because <strong>MSG compromises the immune system</strong>. Research shows MSG suppresses thymus and spleen function <sup><a href="#src-immune">45</a>, <a href="#src-immune-2">46</a>, <a href="#src-immune-3">47</a>, <a href="#src-immune-4">48</a></sup>, creating an invisible vulnerability in the body's natural defenses. Just as Achilles' heel was his hidden weak point, the immune-suppressing effects of MSG represent a hidden vulnerability that most people don't realize exists &mdash; until it's too late.</p>
<p>Anything that may compromise the immune system fits nicely with that Greek story: a hidden weak spot, present from birth (or first exposure), waiting to be struck.</p>
</div>
